\section*{Linear variance of the random 3-SAT unsatisfiability time}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For proper random 3-SAT clauses sampled with replacement, the uncapped first-unsatisfiable hitting time is claimed to have variance $\Theta(n)$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
A new potential sums the probabilities that successive two-clauses kill a given assignment set. This improves the critical occupation estimate in the deletion-delay framework, removes the logarithmic upper-bound loss, and combines cap removal with the established transition-width lower bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For proper random 3-SAT clauses sampled with replacement, the uncapped first-unsatisfiable hitting time is claimed to have variance $\Theta(n)$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 235, the selected entries establish threshold existence, threshold computability, linear variance for $k\geq4$, and the earlier $O(n\log n)$ bound for $k=3$. The later linear three-clause variance improvement is not selected. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

\subsection{Sources}
